==================================================================================================
0. the helpers this task builds on
==================================================================================================
the 24 signed permutations are orthogonal, have determinant +1, contain x,y,z and are closed under composition; the random SO(3) draws the same
windows of 1000 samples at W=128 cover every sample exactly once; macro F1 and the confusion matrix match a case done by hand

==================================================================================================
1. what the 61 recordings are
==================================================================================================
61 recordings, 1122772 samples = 374.3 min, 30 people (users 1..30), experiments 1..61
recordings per user: 29 users with 2, 1 with 3 (user 10)
finite samples 1122772 of 1122772; values in [-2.010, 2.004]
units: over the 360 still-class stretches the measured acceleration has size 1.020 g (sd 0.013, range 0.990..1.046)
      -> the files are in g, not m/s2 (at rest a phone reads about 1 g, and the browser's Sensor gives m/s2, so the app has to convert)
never saturated: |a| is at most 3.39 g anywhere in the 61 recordings
      samples with |a| < 0.2 g (free fall, or a sensor that dropped out): 46 (0.0041%)
      samples with |a| < 0.5 g (free fall, or a sensor that dropped out): 4212 (0.3751%)
      samples with |a| < 0.8 g (free fall, or a sensor that dropped out): 80688 (7.1865%)
      samples equal to the previous one on all three axes: 65338 (5.82%) - 63641 of them a single sample held for one extra sample, only 104 runs longer than 3, and the same 5.5%-6.1% share in every class and every recording. That is the phone's uneven timestamps landed on a regular grid, not a sensor losing movement
sampling rate: 126 walking stretches repeat every 1.12 s (one full stride, 10th-90th pct 1.02-1.22 s), and their spectrum peaks at 1.67 Hz - twice that, the second step of the stride
      -> a 1.1 s stride and a 3.6 s sit-to-stand are human. Read as 100 Hz they would be a 2.2 s stride and a 7.2 s sit-to-stand, and twice as slow again at 200 Hz: the files are at the 50 Hz the description gives

==================================================================================================
2. what the labels are
==================================================================================================
labelled 815614 samples (72.6%); -1 in the pauses 307158 (27.4%)
pauses: 633 stretches, median 279.0 samples (5.6 s), longest 340 s; 511 of them lie between two activities, and every recording also has a median of 8 s before the first label and 337 s after the last
      -> people do not start and stop on cue. Samples with -1 are never scored and never trained on as a class of their own.

class        runs  samples  share  median s   10th   90th  longest
walking       127   122091  15.0%      19.4   16.8   22.1     28.2
upstairs      183   116707  14.3%      12.7   11.1   14.5     17.9
downstairs    186   107961  13.2%      11.8    9.8   13.8     17.6
sitting       120   126677  15.5%      20.8   16.3   27.0     32.4
standing      120   138105  16.9%      22.6   17.3   29.4     40.6
lying         120   136865  16.8%      22.1   17.3   29.7     33.9
transition    358    67208   8.2%       3.6    2.5    5.2      9.9
      -> a transition runs 3.6 s at the median against 20 s for an activity, and is 8.2% of the labelled samples. Six different movements are merged into it, and it is the shortest and rarest class: macro F1 is decided there, which is why the notebook asks for macro F1

the order of the labelled stretches: experiment 1 starts ['stan', 'tran', 'sitt', 'tran', 'stan', 'tran', 'lyin', 'tran', 'sitt'] - activities separated by transitions, as the protocol says. 3 different patterns over the 61 recordings, the commonest shared by 58 of them
recordings without all 7 classes: acc_exp20_user10.txt, acc_exp21_user10.txt (4 classes, 12 label rows, 232 s; 3 classes, 8 label rows, 198 s)
      -> the subject stopped early. Nothing was lost by the parse: over all 61 recordings 0 labelled samples were overwritten by a later label row and 0 were painted past the end of the file
train 41 recordings,  522690 labelled; per class {'walk': 81348, 'upst': 76651, 'down': 70006, 'sitt': 77954, 'stan': 86584, 'lyin': 84770, 'tran': 45377}
valid 10 recordings,  147544 labelled; per class {'walk': 20398, 'upst': 19738, 'down': 18882, 'sitt': 24416, 'stan': 25982, 'lyin': 26305, 'tran': 11823}
test  10 recordings,  145380 labelled; per class {'walk': 20345, 'upst': 20318, 'down': 19073, 'sitt': 24307, 'stan': 25539, 'lyin': 25790, 'tran': 10008}
the baseline the notebook names - always answering standing: test accuracy 0.176, macro F1 0.043 (only standing gets an F1, 0.299); the notebook says about 0.18 and about 0.04

==================================================================================================
3. how the phone is worn: which way gravity points, per recording
==================================================================================================
the mean direction of gravity in the phone's own axes, pooled over the recordings:
  sitting   [0.98  0.133 0.148]
  standing  [ 0.987 -0.158 -0.013]
  lying     [0.078 0.778 0.624]
angles between them: sittin-standi 19 deg, sittin-lying 74 deg, standi-lying 93 deg
  within sitting  , over its 60 recordings: median   9.7 deg from the pooled mean, 90th pct  17.6, worst  30.5
  within standing , over its 60 recordings: median   8.3 deg from the pooled mean, 90th pct  15.2, worst  22.3
  within lying    , over its 60 recordings: median  18.2 deg from the pooled mean, 90th pct  48.9, worst 121.8
the same person's two recordings, same class: a median of 9.4 deg apart (90th pct 37.3, worst 170.8 for user 10 in lying) over 90 pairs -> the phone went back on roughly the same way, not exactly

within one still class the phones point 10 deg apart at the median, while the three still classes sit 19-93 deg apart from each other.
So the dataset does NOT contain the pocket-turning the task is about: the phone was belt-mounted at the waist, nearly the same way on everyone. Any honest test of an orientation-aware method means rotating held-out recordings by hand - which is what train_motion.py will do.

==================================================================================================
4. how much of the task lives in the axis order (nothing learned yet)
==================================================================================================
W = 256 samples (5.1 s): 1944 single-class training windows, 560 test windows whose samples all carry one label (a window that straddles a boundary or sits in a pause has no single answer; the real evaluation is per sample)

nearest-class-average on which way down the window points (3 classes, 145380 -> 257 test windows scored):
  as delivered (x,y,z): accuracy 0.848, macro F1 0.849; per class sitt 0.82, stan 0.82, lyin 0.90
  the 24 turned phones: accuracy 0.047 (worst, -x,-z,-y) to 0.848 (best, x,y,z); 0 of the 23 beat the real orientation
  turning the phone costs a median of 0.553 accuracy - the axis order carries most of what separates sitting from standing from lying

nearest-class-average on mean and sd of each axis (7 classes, 145380 -> 560 test windows scored):
  as delivered (x,y,z): accuracy 0.668, macro F1 0.656; per class walk 0.51, upst 0.35, down 0.68, sitt 0.82, stan 0.80, lyin 0.89, tran 0.53
  the 24 turned phones: accuracy 0.021 (worst, -x,-z,-y) to 0.668 (best, x,y,z); 0 of the 23 beat the real orientation
  turning the phone costs a median of 0.532 accuracy - the axis order carries most of what separates sitting from standing from lying

==================================================================================================
5. what the window length trades off
==================================================================================================
W =   64 samples ( 1.28 s): 11392 training windows on the 41 training recordings,  3.6% of all windows hold two classes,  7.9% hold a transition sample somewhere - the longer the window the more the labels inside it disagree, while the network has to answer each sample separately
W =  128 samples ( 2.56 s):  5688 training windows on the 41 training recordings,  7.2% of all windows hold two classes, 10.0% hold a transition sample somewhere - the longer the window the more the labels inside it disagree, while the network has to answer each sample separately
W =  256 samples ( 5.12 s):  2836 training windows on the 41 training recordings, 14.4% of all windows hold two classes, 14.0% hold a transition sample somewhere - the longer the window the more the labels inside it disagree, while the network has to answer each sample separately
W =  512 samples (10.24 s):  1409 training windows on the 41 training recordings, 28.8% of all windows hold two classes, 21.8% hold a transition sample somewhere - the longer the window the more the labels inside it disagree, while the network has to answer each sample separately
W = 1024 samples (20.48 s):   694 training windows on the 41 training recordings, 57.2% of all windows hold two classes, 35.7% hold a transition sample somewhere - the longer the window the more the labels inside it disagree, while the network has to answer each sample separately

